Apply the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem to

$
  F(x) = integral_a^x f(t) g(t) dif t quad "and" quad G(x) = integral_a^x g(t) dif t:
$

there is $c in (a, b)$ with

$
                    (F(b)-F(a))G'(c) & = (G(b)-G(a))F'(c) \
    g(c) integral_a^b f(t)g(t) dif t & = f(c)g(c) integral_a^b g(t) dif t \
        integral_a^b f(t) g(t) dif t & = f(c) integral_a^b g(t) dif t,
$

where dividing by $G'(c) = g(c) != 0$ is legitimate.
